{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi6ose8B5Cm4N8XGhgCa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The goal's done condition is not met. The game is a playable vertical slice, not the full game TASK.md describes. I built three of the twelve events, and several TASK.md features are missing (listed below). I haven't claimed otherwise.\n\n**What's in the work folder**\n- `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-075524.html`: the single bundled file (engine and game inlined, about 1.27 MB). It loads from `file://` with no console errors.\n- `exit-interview.json`: parses, and fills every field in the required shape.\n- `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-075524/`: the dev folder with `src/game.js`, `tests/run.mjs`, `tools/bundle.mjs` and `tools/shots.py`.\n\n**Choices I made**\n- **Title:** \"Olympiyikes!\" from PLAN.md, since TASK.md asks for the title it gives.\n- **Names:** I used the file-name pattern from STAGE.md (claude-haiku-5-5-medium), not the Opus name in PLAN.md.\n- **Root:** I read \"root\" as the work folder, where STAGE.md sits.\n- **Scope:** the plan describes twelve events and a team build. I built the three that give the best coverage: 100m Dash, Ski Jump and Archery.\n\n**What works and is tested**\n- Title, menu, Single Event, Quick Games (three events in a row, then a medal table), Records, How to Play, and About & Credits.\n- Attract mode: after 10 s idle on the title or menu it plays the three events on a cheating pilot, and any input returns to the title.\n- Dev mode from the pause menu: live sliders for the balance values, a cheat toggle, and reset to defaults. Values persist.\n- `node tests/run.mjs` runs 21 headless checks, including a scripted playthrough of Quick Games, and prints only failures and a count. All 21 pass.\n- Screenshots (`tools/shots.py`) confirmed the title, the three event views, results, the medal table, the About screen and the dev screen.\n\n**What TASK.md asks for that is not built**\n- Nine of the twelve events. Quick Games runs three events, not four.\n- Local multiplayer (1–4 players) and AI Hub ghost heats.\n- Ceremonies, the parade, commentary, and Animalese voices. Sound is the engine's own loops and effects.\n- Mouse aiming, and the on-screen glyph checks for each input.\n- Records with initials and the Laurel and Medal cabinets. Records are one best time or score per event, stored in the browser.\n- The touch-only notice is written, but I didn't test it.\n- The Xbox glyph setting is not verified; I relied on the engine's controls screen.\n- No gamepad was tested; the controls were checked only from the keyboard.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 07:55:24\n- Source: the complete summer and winter Olympic games\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre and perspective: arcade multi-event sports; 2.5D lanes (brawler) for the dash, side view for the ski jump, overhead for archery\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff engine (unchanged copy), plain JavaScript on canvas; Node 24 for tests; Python Playwright with Chromium for screenshots\n- Art direction: engine pixel style, chibi rigs, code-drawn shapes\n- Animation: the engine's Humanoid rig poses\n- Audio: engine chip synth, sound presets and song loops; no audio files, no voices\n- Online: none\n- Testing: headless scripted playthroughs (21 checks) and browser screenshots\n- Research: the engine's own docs only\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium via Playwright\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues caught: six game bugs. Two were critical: attract mode never returned to the title, and the tracks, ramp and archery target drew nothing. Four were smaller: the ski camera sat under the hill, unsupported glyphs showed as \"?\", the results screen read a stale global during a fade, and the medal table compared random numbers with times.\n- Critical or game-breaking: two.\n- Most helpful test: the scripted Quick Games run through the medal table, since it crosses every scene change.\n- Least helpful test: the scene-name checks. They passed while the track was blank, and only screenshots caught that.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the scene and pause-menu setup, and the headless fixed-step advance, which made the tests repeatable.\n- Got stuck or guessed wrong: twice. `E.hex` returns arrays that the pixel helpers reject, so colours silently drew nothing. Scene fades also swallow input, so tests need a wait after each change. The font lacks the middle dot and the controller symbols.\n- Most important thing I built myself: the three event simulations, the attract pilot, the dev sliders and the About screens.\n- Hardest part: knowing which draws are screen space. `r.overlay` paints over the actors, so ground shapes must go in `r.decal` or `r.queue`.\n- Engine changes: none. The engine copy is unchanged.\n- One change I'd make: add the controller symbols and the middle dot to the pixel font, and state each draw call's colour format in the API card.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the 10-foot rules, and the exact file names and deliverable list.\n- Confusing: the plan runs 1700 lines and is written for a team at max effort. It names an Opus folder, and the root folder is not stated clearly.\n- Hardest: fitting twelve events, AI Hub, ceremonies and voices into one session.\n- Change: mark a first-pass scope by priority, name the root folder, and trim the plan to what one session can build.\n\nTo finish, the remaining nine events, the four-event Quick Games, local multiplayer, the AI Hub integration, ceremonies and voices, and the records cabinets would each need building and testing. I stopped at this point because that is more than one session's work."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":92